1. Fire spread model uses fuel type (grass/forest/dense), wind direction (dot product against spread vector), wind speed multiplier, moisture suppression, 
temperature multiplier, and diagonal falloff. Structures burn slower than dense forest. Water/road act as natural firebreaks.

2. Also take into account ember spotting, based on wind direction.

3. Fire spreads according to the 30-30-30 rule. when temperatures are above 30°C, relative humidity is 30% or lower, and winds are blowing at 30 km/h or higher, 
fires can burn uncontrollably.

4. Fire can start from anywhere, not just the corners of the screen. Progressive levels could have a probability distribution over the map describing how likely it 
is that a fire can start in that region. Designate deeper reds to high probability regions, and diminish radially.
